<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/nvalt-tagging-rb.7542.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>Tags in nvALT used to be a string you typed into a text field. They are tokens now, and the search field knows what a tag is.</p>

<h3 id="tokens-instead-of-a-tag-string">Tokens instead of a tag string</h3>

<p>Click the tags on a note (or hit <span class="keycombo separated" title="Shift-Command-T"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8679;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">T</kbd></span>) and you get a token field. Each tag is its own token. Type a new one, hit the separator (space, comma), and it becomes a token too. The same field is what you get when you tag a single note from the list.</p>

<p>nvALT already had tag autocompletion from your existing tags, so that comes forward with these other improvements.</p>

<h3 id="tagging-more-than-one-note">Tagging more than one note</h3>

<p>Select multiple notes and open the tag panel (again <span class="keycombo separated" title="Shift-Command-T"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8679;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">T</kbd></span>). You see every tag those notes carry, not just the ones they all share.</p>

<p>Tags that are on every selected note stay highlighted. Tags that only some of the notes have show up dimmed, so you can tell a shared tag from a stray one. Apply a dimmed tag by clicking it and it gets added to the notes that were missing it. Take a highlighted tag away and it comes off the whole selection. It&rsquo;s very close to how Finder tagging works.</p>

<h3 id="no-more-openmeta">No More OpenMeta</h3>

<p>This was actually a change in an earlier release, but it bears repeating. nvALT saves tags on notes to tags on the file itself, if you have plain text storage enabled. Previously this used OpenMeta tags, which were awesome in their day (over a decade ago), but not so much today, now that Apple has sherlocked them and Finder offers its own tags. nvALT now uses Finder tags, so you can search by tag from Spotlight<sup id="fnref:spotlight"><a href="https://brettterpstra.com#fn:spotlight" class="footnote" rel="footnote" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>.</p>

<h3 id="tag-in-the-search-field"><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#tag</code> in the search field</h3>

<p>The search field now treats a word that starts with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#</code> as a tag. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#unix</code> lists notes whose tags start with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unix</code>. Case does not matter, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#uni</code> is enough to catch <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unix</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unix-admin</code> while you are still typing.</p>

<p>It mixes with a normal search. Every piece has to match.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>#unix shell
#unix #mac</code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#unix shell</code> means a tag starting with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unix</code>, and the note also matches <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">shell</code>. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#unix #mac</code> means both tags. The hash has to sit against the word, so <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"># unix</code> is just a hash character plus a search for <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">unix</code>.</p>

<p>I might evenutally add functionality that will search your notes for <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#tags</code> in the content and apply them as tags to the note, but not yet.</p>

<h3 id="web-clipping">Web clipping</h3>

<p>I personally think saving the text of a web page you searched for and which answered a question in a searchable database of your own is just prudent record keeping. Its why I build so many &ldquo;markdownifier&rdquo; options (Marky, Marked&rsquo;s URL import, gather, etc.). I built it into nvALT years ago, but it had since stopped working for most people. So I rebuilt it.</p>

<p>Dragging a URL onto the notes list still works. Most browsers do not give you a proxy icon you can drag anymore, so Note → Import URL… opens a field for the address. If the clipboard is a single web address, that address is already filled in. A host with no scheme is fine. nvALT adds <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">https://</code>.</p>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/nvalt-web-clipper-prefs-800.jpg"></p>

<p>Turn on Convert imported URLs to Markdown and the page comes in as Markdown. Process with Readability extracts the article first. Hold Option when you choose Import URL, or Option-drag a URL onto the list, and Readability runs for that import even if the preference is off.</p>

<p>The new note is selected as soon as it arrives. The search field is set to the note&rsquo;s title, so a leftover search does not hide it.</p>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nvalt://markdownify</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nv://markdownify</code> do the same conversion for a script or a browser extension. Pass the page as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">url</code>, or the HTML itself as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">html</code> (then <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">url</code> is only the source link). <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">title</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">readability</code> (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">0</code>) are optional. This scheme always writes Markdown, even when Convert imported URLs to Markdown is off.</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>nvalt://markdownify?url=https://brettterpstra.com
nvalt://markdownify?html=...&amp;url=https://brettterpstra.com&amp;readability=1</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The converter is built into the app now with my own Swift libraries. It uses the same HTML2Text and Readability setup as Marked, instead of bundled Python scripts.</p>

<h4 id="browser-extensions">Browser Extensions</h4>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/safari-extension-nvalt.jpg"></p>

<p>Long ago, nvALT had a browser extension for at least Chrome, and maybe Firefox. They weren&rsquo;t maintained anymore, so I&rsquo;ve built new ones. They use the same framework as the <a href="https://markedapp.com/extensions/">Marked extensions</a>, allowing you to markdownify a whole URL (with or without Readability to remove distractions), a current selection on the page, or select one or more blocks using a hover highlight and click (<span class="keycombo separated" title="Command"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd></span> or <span class="keycombo separated" title="Shift-left click"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8679;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol"><svg class="key-pointer" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="0.75em" height="0.95em" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.15em"><path fill="currentColor" d="M2 1.2 2 13.2 5.7 9.6 8.6 15.2 11.3 13.8 8.4 8.2 13.4 7.6Z"></path></svg></kbd></span> for multiple blocks).</p>

<p>The Safari extension ships inside the latest version of nvALT. The Chrome/Brave and Firefox/Zen extensions will require approval from the respective stores, and that can take a while. But if you&rsquo;re curious, I&rsquo;m <a href="https://github.com/ttscoff/nvalt-extension">putting the code up on GitHub</a>.</p>

